---
title: "New Filters Tool Panel"
enterprise: true
---

The new **Filters Tool Panel** allows accessing the grid's filters without needing to open up the column menu.

## Enabling New Filters Tool Panel

{% gridExampleRunner title="New Filters Tool Panel" name="simple" /%}

To use the new Filters Tool Panel, set `enableFilterHandlers = true`, and use the `'filters-new'` [Side Bar Configuration](./side-bar/#configuring-the-side-bar).

If a filter is set from anywhere outside of the tool panel, e.g. from the column menu, the filter will automatically be added to the tool panel. Once a filter appears in the tool panel, it will remain until it's removed from within the tool panel. Removing a filter from the tool panel also clears that filter.

## Using Buttons

The tool panel can be configured to have global [Apply, Clear, Reset and Cancel Buttons](./filter-applying/#apply-clear-reset-and-cancel-buttons). These behave the same as when used within an individual filter, but instead apply to all filters at once.

To use global buttons, every filter must first be configured to use the buttons. The global buttons can then be passed to the tool panel configuration when using `agNewFiltersToolPanel`, by passing an `IToolPanelNewFiltersCompParams` object:

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="IToolPanelNewFiltersCompParams" config={"description": ""} /%}

The example below demonstrates using buttons with the tool panel:

{% gridExampleRunner title="New Filters Tool Panel Buttons" name="using-buttons" /%}

Note that when opened from the column menu, the buttons will be visible within the individual filters. Within the tool panel, only the global buttons will be visible.

## Configuring Filters

### Changing Selectable Filters

For each column filter, the filter type can be changed by using the dropdown within the tool panel. To enable switching filters, set `filter: 'agSelectableColumnFilter'` on the column definition. For any other `filter` value, the dropdown will not be displayed.

By default, the dropdown will allow switching between:

- `Simple Filter` - [Text Filter](./filter-text/), [Number Filter](./filter-number/), [BigInt Filter](./filter-bigint/) or [Date Filter](./filter-date/), depending on the [Cell Data Type](./cell-data-types/)
- `Selection Filter` - [Set Filter](./filter-set)
- `Combo Filter` - [Multi Filter](./filter-multi) (if the `MultiFilterModule` is loaded)

To allow other filters to be configured in the dropdown, `filterParams` of type `SelectableFilterParams` can be set on the column definition:

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="SelectableFilterParams" config={"description": ""} /%}

### Suppressing Filters

To remove a particular column / filter from the tool panel, set the column property `suppressFiltersToolPanel` to `true`.

{% apiDocumentation source="column-properties/properties.json" section="filtering" names=["suppressFiltersToolPanel"] /%}

### Example: Configuring Filters

The example below demonstrates configuring filters:

- The **Athlete** column does not show a dropdown as it has `filter: 'agSetColumnFilter'` set.
- The **Age** column shows all three grid-provided filter options by default.
- The **Country** column is configured to show the Set Filter before the Text Filter.
- The **Year** column is configured to show a custom filter component with the Set Filter.
- The **Date** column has `suppressFiltersToolPanel: true` set, so it does not show in the tool panel.

{% gridExampleRunner title="New Filters Tool Panel Configuration" name="configuring-filters" /%}

## Filters Tool Panel State

When the grid initialises, the tool panel only shows active filters provided in the [Initial State](./grid-state/) filter model. The tool panel can also be configured via initial state to show inactive filters, or change the order and expansion state. This is provided to the Side Bar state as a `NewFiltersToolPanelState` object:

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="NewFiltersToolPanelState" config={"description": ""} /%}

### Managing Tool Panel State

The filter cards shown in the tool panel are part of the [Side Bar](./side-bar/) state, which is separate from the filter model. Clearing the filter model with `api.setFilterModel(null)` therefore clears the filter values but leaves the (now inactive) cards in place.

To remove cards from the tool panel programmatically, see [Set Filters](#set-filters).

A saved layout can be restored by setting the `sideBar` state to a previously captured one. `setState` resets any part of the grid state it is not given, so spread the current state to keep everything else (e.g. sorting) unchanged. Replace the `filter` state too to restore each card's values as well as the cards themselves. See [Grid State](./grid-state/) for the full `getState` and `setState` semantics.

The example below demonstrates these operations:

- **Clear Filter Values** calls `api.setFilterModel(null)`. The rows are unfiltered, but the cards remain.
- **Restore Initial State** sets the Side Bar state back to the state the grid was created with.
- **Save State** captures the current grid state with `api.getState()`.
- **Restore Saved State** applies the saved `filter` and `sideBar` state, restoring both the cards and their values.

{% gridExampleRunner title="New Filters Tool Panel State" name="state" /%}

## Expand / Collapse Filters

The filter cards in the tool panel can be expanded and collapsed by invoking methods on the Filters Tool Panel instance:

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="INewFiltersToolPanel" names=["expandFilters","collapseFilters"] config={"description":""} /%}

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
// expands the 'age' and 'country' filter cards
api.getToolPanelInstance('filters-new').expandFilters(['age', 'country']);
```

Only filters which already have a card in the tool panel are affected.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Expand / Collapse Filters" name="expand-collapse-filters" /%}

## Set Filters

`setFilters` sets exactly which filter cards are shown in the tool panel, and in what order. **Any column omitted from the list has its card removed, and its filter is cleared.** Passing an empty array (`[]`) removes every card and clears their filters.

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="INewFiltersToolPanel" names=["setFilters","getState"] config={"description":""} /%}

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
// shows only the 'athlete', 'country' and 'year' cards, in that order
api.getToolPanelInstance('filters-new').setFilters(['athlete', 'country', 'year']);
```

- Cards are shown in the order given.
- Cards that already exist keep their expanded state. Cards that are added are collapsed, unlike when added using **Add Filter** in the tool panel, which adds them expanded.
- Column IDs that do not match a column in the grid are skipped, and a warning is logged in development.

### Removing Only Inactive Cards

To remove only the cards that have no active filter, use `getState` to get the current cards and pass back those that have a filter:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const toolPanel = api.getToolPanelInstance('filters-new');
const colIds = (toolPanel.getState().filters ?? []).map(({ colId }) => colId);
toolPanel.setFilters(colIds.filter((colId) => api.getColumnFilterModel(colId) != null || api.getColumnFilterModel(colId, true) != null));
```

This keeps cards with an applied filter, and cards with unapplied changes (when [buttons](#using-buttons) are used).

{% note %}
`api.refreshToolPanel()` currently re-applies the cached initial card list, which can undo the result of `setFilters`. This is a known issue.
{% /note %}

The example below demonstrates setting filters:

- **Show Athlete, Country, Year** calls `setFilters(['athlete', 'country', 'year'])`. The Age card is removed and its filter is cleared.
- **Reverse Order** reverses the order of the current cards.
- **Remove Inactive Cards** removes the cards that have no filter.
- **Remove All Cards** calls `setFilters([])`. Every card is removed and the rows are unfiltered.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Set Filters" name="set-filters" /%}

## Filter Instances

The filters provided in the tool panel are the same instances as the filter in the column menu. This has the following implications:

- Configuration relating to filters equally applies when the filters appear in the tool panel.
- The filter behaves exactly as when it appears in the column menu. E.g. the Apply button will have the same meaning when used in the tool panel. Also the relationship with the Floating Filter (if active) will be the same.
- If the filter is open on the tool panel and then the user subsequently opens the column menu, the tool panel filter will be closed. Because the filter is the same filter instance, it will only appear at one location at any given time.
